# Example GitHub Actions workflows

Pick the file that matches your project, copy it to
`.github/workflows/drift-sync.yml` (or `drift-check.yml` for verify-only), and
adjust the marked spots (extraction command, spec paths).

| File | Stack | Mode |
|------|-------|------|
| `go-huma.yml` | Go + huma | sync + PR |
| `go-gin-swaggo.yml` | Go + gin (swaggo annotations) | sync + PR |
| `python-fastapi.yml` | Python + FastAPI | sync + PR |
| `node-nestjs.yml` | Node + NestJS | sync + PR |
| `java-spring.yml` | Java + Spring (springdoc) | sync + PR |
| `generic-files.yml` | Any — you already generate a spec file | sync + PR |
| `verify-only.yml` | Any — verify only, no auto-fix | check (fails CI on breaking) |

## Two modes

- **Sync + PR** (`drift-sync.yml`): on every push, detect drift, fix the
  published spec to match the code, and open a pull request for review. Nothing
  is merged automatically — the PR is the human approval gate.
- **Verify only** (`drift-check.yml`): on every push/PR, fail CI if the docs
  have drifted. No changes are made. Add it to branch protection to block merges
  with breaking drift.

You can use both: verify on PRs, sync on `main`.

## How the PR gets its body

The sync workflows run `driftsync diff --format md` to write a markdown drift
report to `drift-report.md`, then pass it to `create-pull-request` via
`body-path`. So the PR description *is* the drift report — a table of what
changed, breaking items first. Don't delete `drift-report.md` before the PR
step; the `Apply fixes` step removes the generated spec but keeps the report.

The report file is never committed — `create-pull-request` only commits the spec
change, so the report shows up as the PR body, not as a file in the diff.

## The only stack-specific part

Every workflow is identical except **Step 1 — extracting a spec from your
code**. That's the one piece that depends on your framework. Everything after it
(diff → apply → PR) is the same for all stacks, because driftsync only ever
compares two OpenAPI files.

## Secrets (optional)

For LLM-written descriptions of new fields, add repo secrets:

- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`
- `OPENAI_API_KEY` (used as fallback if Anthropic fails)

Without them, the pipeline still runs — new-field descriptions are just left
blank.